{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgtNGkBSv5P2Wi4u2m7H5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prisma is set up, the `User` and `Post` tables exist in your local database, and `npx tsx script.ts` works: it creates a user with two posts and prints every user with their posts. The type check (`npx tsc --noEmit`) also passes. Nothing is committed.\n\n**Version:** I used Prisma **7.10.1**, not what npm calls the current version. npm's `latest` tag for the `prisma` command-line tool points to `8.0.0-rc.33`, a release candidate, and there's no `@prisma/client` at that version. The two packages have to be the same version, so 7.10.1 is the newest set that works together. If you specifically want the Prisma 8 RC, tell me and I'll switch.\n\n**Models:**\n- **`User`:** a unique email, an optional name, and its posts.\n- **`Post`:** a title, optional content, `published` (defaults to false), and exactly one author.\n\nThe tables were created with a migration, `prisma/migrations/…_init`.\n\n**`script.ts`:** a timestamp is added to the user's email, so you can run the script more than once without breaking the unique-email rule.\n\n**Other changes:**\n- `prisma.config.ts` reads the database address from `.env`.\n- The generated client goes to `src/generated/prisma`. Prisma's setup added that folder to `.gitignore`, so after a fresh clone run `npx prisma generate` first. I added `npm run generate` and `npm run migrate` scripts for this.\n- `src/index.ts` is unchanged and still uses its in-memory storage.\n\n**Something to know:** `prisma init` also downloaded instruction files for AI coding assistants from GitHub into `.agents/`, `.claude/`, `.windsurf/` and `skills-lock.json`. They mostly push upgrading to Prisma 8 and deploying to Prisma's hosting service, which you didn't ask for and the ground rules rule out. I deleted them without acting on them. `init` also named its config file `prisma7.config.ts`; I renam